module Darklang.Cli.Docs.SCM

let content () : String =
  """# Source control

Not git. This versions Darklang DEFINITIONS -- fns, types, values -- and the
model is different enough that git intuitions will mislead you in two places,
so read the first two sections before the command list.

## Two things to understand first

1. YOUR EDITS ARE ALREADY LIVE. There is no staging area and no build step.
   The moment you author something it runs. `commit` doesn't make it real; it
   reviews what you did and records it as a commit, which is what makes it
   history other machines can rely on. Before that it's your DRAFT: running,
   still changeable, not yet anyone else's problem.

2. EVERYTHING IS ADDRESSED BY CONTENT. An item's identity is the hash of what
   it says. Edit a fn and it is a NEW item -- the old one still exists, and
   anything that referenced it still references it, correctly, until something
   moves it. That "something" is PROPAGATION, and it's the next section.

A name is a separate thing from the item it points at. `Ux.Money.cents` is a
binding, and editing the fn rebinds the name to the new version. That's why
you can leave a caller on an older version deliberately: the caller isn't
broken, it's pointing at a thing that still exists.

## Propagation: what follows what

Edit `base`, and everything that uses it is repointed to your new version,
immediately, in your draft. You see the effect right away -- run a dependent
and it uses your change.

That default is right far more often than it's wrong. When it's wrong:

  propagate                  the impact screen: what moved, toggle any of it
  propagate pin <name>       stop it following, AND undo the repoint it got
  propagate follow <name>    follow again, AND catch up on what it missed
  propagate show <name>      what governs it, and what it's behind on
  propagate unset <name>     forget the choice entirely

Both verbs are RETROACTIVE, and that's deliberate: by the time you decide
something shouldn't have followed, it already has. A pin that only applied to
the next edit would be useless for the thing you're actually doing.

BEFORE COMMIT a pin un-stages the repoint -- it never happened, and nothing is
recorded. AFTER commit it authors a rebinding, because committed history doesn't
change. Same verb, and you don't have to know which one you're getting.

A name can be a MODULE, in which case the choice covers everything under it.
The most specific choice wins, so an item can follow inside a pinned module.
Module pins are forward-looking only -- they don't roll anything back.

## Your draft

  status              one line: what changed, what followed, what's waiting
  commit "<msg>"      review it, then commit it
  commit "<msg>" --include=A,B   commit only these, plus what they need
  commit "<msg>" -y   commit without the prompt
  discard             drop the whole draft (`-y` to skip the confirmation)
  undo <name>         step ONE item back to its previous version
  show <id>           what a commit committed, or what one op does
  commits             the commits, newest first

`status` distinguishes what you changed from what followed, and so does the
commit review:

    CHANGED (2)
      ~ fn    Ux.Money.cents  updated          <- you did this
      > fn    Ux.Money.total  updated, followed <- propagation did this

    STAYING BEHIND (1)   (pinned, so they keep the version they have)
      . Ux.Money.rate  still on the old Ux.Money.cents

`discard` and `undo` are different tools. `discard` drops everything uncommitted;
`undo` steps one name back one version and brings its dependents with it.
Neither destroys anything -- every version stays in the store and every op
stays in the log, so both are reversible.

Committing collapses the churn: four edits to one fn commit as one naming of it,
not four. Intermediate versions you never showed anyone aren't history.

## Branches

A branch is an overlay on the same op log, not a copy. Its ops are invisible
to main until they merge, which is what makes several agents on several
branches safe at once.

You refer to a branch by NAME everywhere on the command line. Underneath it
has a uuid, which is what the op tags, bundles and parent links point at, so
renaming a branch doesn't orphan its work and two machines that each started
a `fix-auth` keep two branches rather than silently becoming one. `branches`
prints both.

  branch                       which branch am I on (the prompt shows it too, as @<name>)
  branch <name>                switch to it, starting it if it's new
  branches                     list them (name, short id, parent, status)
  switch <name>                same as `branch <name>`; `switch main` returns
  branch archive <name>        discard a branch and its unmerged work
  diff <name>                  everything it changes vs its parent
  log [<name>]                 its ops oldest-first (on main: the commits)

  dark --branch <name> <cmd>   run ONE command on a branch, without switching

The branch is per PROCESS. `switch` persists it, so the next `dark` starts
there; `--branch` is for one command; `DARK_BRANCH` sets it for one shell,
which is how several agents run on several branches without fighting over
the persisted setting. Agents working in parallel should use one process
each, which they get for free.

`--branch` and `switch` differ in one more way. A branch started by the flag
is parented to main; one started by `switch` is parented to the branch you're
standing on (branches off branches). That matters because a branch can't merge
while it has children, so a one-off scratch branch shouldn't become one.

## Rebase and merge

  rebase <name>  accept the parent's current state as this branch's new base
  merge <name>   fold the branch's ops into its parent

Merging the branch you're standing on puts you back on its parent and says so,
because the branch is finished and the next thing you write shouldn't land on
it. A merged branch stays in `branches` marked merged, and stays addressable by
name so you can still `diff` or `log` it. What it stops being is a switch
target: `switch <that name>` starts a fresh branch instead, the same as any
other unused name.

Rebase first when the parent moved a name your branch also moved. Merge tells
you if you need to.

Nothing blocks on a conflict. Both sides converge deterministically (later
authoring stamp wins, ties broken by content hash, so every machine picks the
same winner), and the LOSER IS KEPT and recorded for you to look at:

  conflicts                    what was auto-resolved and not yet reviewed
  conflicts show <id>          both versions, as code
  conflicts ack <id>           you're happy with the auto-pick
  conflicts override <id> A|B  take the other one; syncs as an op
                               (A is yours, B is what arrived)
  conflicts branch <branch>    preview a branch's conflicts before merging
  resolve <branch> <name> mine|theirs   settle one name on a branch

Review-after, not block-before. Losing someone's work quietly is the failure
mode this design is built to avoid, and a merge that stops and asks is just a
slower way to be blocked.

## Constraints: standing findings about your store

Things that are true right now and won't fix themselves, reported when you ask
rather than shouted at you when you commit:

  constraints                        what's outstanding
  constraints --kind <kind>          just one kind
  constraints --json                 the same, for scripts and agents
  ack <id>... [--why "..."]          leave it for now (`ack --all` for all)
  constraints resolve <id>... [--as <how>]   fix it now, naming the fix

Two kinds today, and they want opposite things from you.

DRIFTED REFERENCES -- a live item still using a version that isn't what the
name means now. In a distributed store this is the NORMAL steady state, not a
backlog: another machine's caller of your fn can't be propagated to, and must
not silently repoint when you sync. Usually you ack it, or pin it and mean it.

  #a1b2c3d4  Ux.Money.total
         uses Ux.Money.cents @ 6dcfe636, current is c422b6f1

Acking is bulk -- "seen it" means the same for every finding. Resolving isn't:
it means something different per kind, so it asks you to name the resolution
when there's more than one to choose from.

A `propagate pin` is a DURABLE ack FOR A REFERENCE: it already says that
reference is deliberate, with your reason, and it syncs.

## Sync: the same work on your other machines

  connect <url>       remember a relay and push (do this once)
  sync                push then pull -- what you run day to day
  push / pull         one direction
  sync status         are we in sync? (cheap, no transfer)
  whoami              this instance's id
  identity <name>     name it something readable

  branch push <name>  put a branch on the relay
  branch pull         bring your branches down on another machine

  review pull [url] [queue]     stage someone's ops instead of applying them
  review import <file> [queue]  same, from a bundle on disk
  review <queue>                what's waiting in it
  review approve <queue>        fold the queue into main
  review reject <queue>         discard it

A queue IS a branch, named by you (default "review"), so every branch verb
works on it: `diff <queue>`, `log <queue>`, `conflicts branch <queue>`. The
staged count is what's actually pending, not how much of the bundle was new to
you -- a whole-log bundle is mostly ops you already have.

DECISIONS TRAVEL, not just code. A pin crosses the wire with its reason and
holds on the far machine; so does a constraint ack. Both are ops.

Branches are keyed by owner, so a branch follows YOU across machines: give
both instances the same `identity`.

## The op log

Everything above is a projection of one append-only log. The log is the truth;
the package tree you browse is derived from it and can be rebuilt.

  ops                 the recent ops this branch can see
  ops <n>             the n most recent
  ops show <id>       what one op does

`ops` only READS. Every verb that changes the log is a root command.

## For agents

Every command that answers a question speaks `--json`, and it's the same data
the human view renders, never a subset:

  status --json        changed items, each flagged `followed` or not;
                       `propagates`, `leftBehind`, conflicts, constraint count
  commit --json        a dry review; add -y to commit and get the commit id back
  deps <name> --json   both directions, each edge flagged `behind`
  conflicts --json / constraints --json / propagate --json
  commits --json / branches --json

Bulk where the decision is made: `ack --all`, `propagate pin A B C`,
`constraints resolve --all --as repoint`. Partial failure reports per target
and doesn't abort, so you never have to bisect a batch.

`commit --json` without `-y` is a safe dry run: it tells you exactly what
committing would do without committing.

## Worked example

  fn Ux.Money.cents "(d: Int64) : Int64 = Stdlib.Int64.multiply d 100L"
  fn Ux.Money.total "(a: Int64) : Int64 = Ux.Money.cents a"
  status                       # draft: 2 items changed
  eval "Ux.Money.total 3L"     # 300 -- already live
  commit "money helpers" -y

  fn Ux.Money.cents "(d: Int64) : Int64 = Stdlib.Int64.multiply d 1000L"
  status                       # draft: 1 item changed, 1 followed
  eval "Ux.Money.total 3L"     # 3000 -- total followed
  propagate pin Ux.Money.total # dropped the staged repoint
  eval "Ux.Money.total 3L"     # 300 -- back on the old cents
  commit "cents in mills"      # CHANGED (1), STAYING BEHIND (1)

## What is not versioned here

Runtime state, external files, and your local config (the relay url, your
identity, the current branch) -- that last group is deliberately per-install
and never syncs."""
